In article <blcpt4$pi7$1@online.de>, helbig@astro.multiCLOTHESvax.de (Phillip Helbig---remove CLOTHES to reply) writes:

:There seem to be 3 types of connections on the machines: 3 rather big
:holes with pins inside them,

  That one, I've seen on TURBOchannel hardware...

:                               something that looks like a monitor
:connector on a PC (maybe it is the same thing), and a 15-pin connector
:(which looks like the one used for a keyboard/mouse combination thing
:where each plugs into a block on a cord and the cord plugs in to the
:15-pin connector on the machine). 

  Check the FAQ to start with, for general information on this topic.

  As for the interfaces, newer stuff uses VGA-compatible connections
  with either the 15-pin D connector or with the five BNC connection.
  The three BNC connection is moderately compatible with generic
  monitors, but requires the monitor support synchronization signals
  on the green wire (synch-on-green) -- the five BNC wiring variant 
  has seperate horizontal (h-synch) and vertical (v-synch) signals.

:Can I assume that any combination will work if I have the correct cable?

  With newer systems, generally yes.  With older systems, compatability
  is rather less likely without a video signal adapter -- if one is 
  available.  Please see the FAQ.

:Will a "standard PC monitor" work with any VAX or ALPHA I might have?

  With newer systems, generally yes.  With older systems, again, 
  please see the FAQ.

:Can I damage anything by trying out combinations which won't work?

  Donno.  I'd tend to doubt it -- but wiring weirdness being what it
  is, some sort of damage is certainly conceivable.  (I remember one
  tradeshow where we were seeing ground potential differences between
  the power for the displays and for the the servers, and we were 
  getting monstrous sparks on the video shields.  But I digress.)
  *Usually*, you won't damage anything with swapping plugs -- but
  you can under- or over-drive the monitors, and there is no
  guarantee that the pinouts and the signals will match.

:Are there any other differences besides the obvious physical ones?

  Over-driving the monitor can fry it.  There are both pin-out and
  signal-level differences for the keyboard and mouse protocols, 
  too.  There are likely other differences.

  If you want to pursue this, we'll need to know the particular
  graphics controllers involved in this discussion...


