On 2003-09-23, David J. Dachtera <djesys.nospam@fsi.net> wrote:
> Do you have a desoldering tool? Spring-loaded sucker - heat the joint
> until the solder flows then suck it away. Let it cool and break teh pin
> loose with a small screw driver or something else. Do that on each pin,
> being careful to cool the board so it doesn't heat the surrounding
> components. Kinda tedious, but...

If you can pull the pins individually, my approach would be to heat the
joint from the solder side while pulling on the pin with pliers from the
component side, THEN suck any remaining solder out of the hole. Probably
faster that way, and less likely to lift a pad while breaking the pins
loose.

The stubborn pins are probably hooked to the power/ground planes, you'll
need a fairly hot iron to pull those.

-- uns


