Excellent!  I'm going away, and thought I would have to delay posting
this until I got back for a trip I'm taking...

This is the first public announcement of my XY Display Simulator
project.  Right now I handle simple point plotting (ie; PDP-1 Type 30)
and the PDP-11 VT11 display processor used in the GT40.

The simulator can be used standalone -- one test program is the famous
Display Hack "Munching Squares" first discovered on the PDP-1.

I have plugged my simulator into SIMH's PDP-1 and PDP-11 simulators,
but others are possible, including the PDP-8 VC08E and AX08
interfaces, and I've started on a "Type 340" display processor
simulator in the hope of running PDP-10 spacewar (likely to be the
first 340 ever hooked to a KS10), and my code should be also be usable
with other CPU simulators (the coordinate system and sizing are most
friendly to DEC systems, but others should be possible).

My little project's home page is

   http://www.ultimate.com/phil/xy/

and includes screen shots of PDP-1 spacewar and PDP-11 lunar lander,
along with a tarball which includes my simulator and files to use it
with SIMH v3.0-2 (see above), and binaries for spacewar and lunar
lander.  Both games are playable.

The standalone tests should build and run under any Un*xish system
with X11, and also build under Win32 with MINGW or Cygwin. Recent
versions of VMS with X11 should be possible, but I don't know what the
build procedure might entail. I have not tested the SIMH makefile
under Win32, but it should either work, or require only minor tweaks.

Areas for input/improvement:

I'd be interested in feedback from anyone who has, or has used actual
Type 30 or VT11/VR14 based displays, to hear if the simulations look
and feel right (in terms of timing, and the color decay of the screen
"phosphor", etc).

The VT11 simulator hasn't been completely wrung out (I've haven't yet
figured out how to load and run the "GT" file format diagnostics
available -- anyone have a GT to LDA converter?), and some little
artifacts appear when running lunar lander.

The window system support code for both X11 and Win32 is probably
apalling to anyone schooled in the art. After over 15 years using
(first X10 and now) X11, and programming on Unix, this is the first
program I've ever written that so much as opens a window!


