Kees,

Sorry!  I looked inside the chassis of mine.  The main board has only 6 SIMM
sockets.  It needs identical pairs of SIMMs, 70 ns or faster, fast-page mode
with true parity.  (There was a time when SIMMs were expensive and manufacturers
produced SIMMs without the 9th parity chip, substituting a tiny ASIC which
generated the parity for each access to memory on the SIMM.)  Allowable memory
capacities, if you can still find them, are 4MB (useless), 8MB, 16MB, 32MB.
Only certain combinations are allowed, probably the same ones as for the
Celebris XL Pentium-class system for which electronic documentation still can be
found.

At this very late date, I am unsure as to which graphics cards work with the
Alpha XL266.  Surely it is limited by BOTH hardware and software.  The mainboard
was designed and produced early enough so that it probably meets only the
earliest PCI 1.0 specification, possibly 1.1.   A Trio64 card may be too new.
In the S3 family of cards, I would recommend the S3968 chip or something
earlier.

Sorry about the mixup about numbers of SIMMs.  I got confused with the Pentium
Pro processor card for the same chassis and mainboard.  The PPro card has its
own eight SIMM sockets, and different memory configuration rules... Ben Myers

