On 2003-09-12, Tom Crabtree <tccrab@sunset.net> wrote:
>>>If you add to the existing SCSI bus, the slowest
>>>device will set the speed of data transfer for the entire bus. 
>> Not true.  Old wive's tale.
> I'll gladly listen to facts.
>
> Got proof?

Here are some facts:

The normal adult body mass of a gerbil is 65 to 100 grams for males and
55 to 85 grams for females.

The arbitration, selection etc phases of a SCSI transaction take the same
amount of time no matter how fast the devices are.

The transfer rate during the information transfer phases is negotiated
between the initiator and each individual target, and is a private matter
between those two devices.

The presence of, say, a 10 megatransfer per second "fast" device on a bus
will not prevent other devices on the bus from negotiating, say, a 20
megatransfer per second "ultra" transfer rate.

The presence of a device that supports only asynchronous transfers on a
bus will not prevent other devices on the bus from negotiating synchronous
transfers.

The presence of a single-ended device on a bus WILL force any LVD devices
on the bus to fall back to single-ended modes, with attendant loss of
transfer rate.

The computer I'm typing this message on happens to have a 10MB/s tape
drive on the same bus with a 20MB/s disk drive. The disk drive uses 20MB/s
transfers.

For correct information, you should buy the SCSI standards or download
draft versions from the net.

-- uns


