rhoop@sbcglobal.net (RHOOP) writes:
> I'm trying to find information on getting off my ThickNet Backbone and
> onto my newer IP Backbone.  I have DECServer 200s connected to DELNIs
> which connect to the Old Ethernet, I'm assuming they talk LAT.  Can I
> use a Cisco Router in here somewhere and talk LAT across my new
> Backbone?  Would I replace the DS 200s with a Access Router or is
> there a way to connect all the DS 200s to a Router instead of the
> DELNI?

You can to use a 10Base-T transceiver to connect a DECserver to a newer
network (instead of 10Base-5 "thicknet"), but LAT isn't a routable
protocol, so the LAT traffic will only make it to hosts on the same
layer two network, i.e., using Etherenet hubs, switches, and bridges.
Can your Cisco be configured to bridge LAT?

If you really need a terminal server that will work over an IP network,
you need to get a newer DEC model that supports TCP/IP, or a different
brand of terminal server.


