"RHOOP" <rhoop@sbcglobal.net> schreef in bericht
news:788826a0.0309121159.7e15b3c4@posting.google.com...
> I'm trying to find information on getting off my ThickNet Backbone and
> onto my newer IP Backbone.  I have DECServer 200s connected to DELNIs
> which connect to the Old Ethernet, I'm assuming they talk LAT.  Can I
> use a Cisco Router in here somewhere and talk LAT across my new
> Backbone?  Would I replace the DS 200s with a Access Router or is
> there a way to connect all the DS 200s to a Router instead of the
> DELNI?

There are many questions and pitfalls in your short pice of text!
You mention IP backbone and ThickNet backbone in the same sentence. You're
mixing up a protocol (IP) with a network technology (thickwire). Thickwire
ethernet can carry all protocols that run over thinwire, UTP, fiber, you
name it. Its only restriction is its speed: 10 Mb/s (half duplex obviously).
LAT is a protocol and runs over ethernet, FDDI and tokenring as well as any
combination of those. Provided you have the equipment to connect between
different LAN infrastructures. E.g. we used a Cisco MGS router with the
(then optionally) bridging software to connect thickwire, thinwire ethernet
and a 16 Mb/s tomenring net. DECnet and LAT ran on all media and IP was kept
on tokenring only.

A DS200 has an AUI interface only. It plugs straight into a DELNI or an
H4000 or H4005 transceiver. Alternatively you can put a small Allied Telesis
transceiver directly to the AUI port. These A.T. transceivers are
inexpensive. A DEC DESTA transceiver also works but blocks port 1, which is
by default the console port and you might want access to that. The DESTA is
more expensive than an A.T. but has a near infinite lifespan compared to the
A.T. products. Of course you can change the console function to another port
but you loose one port out of eight. This way your DS200 (and DS100) will
still connect to modern UTP or even fiber networks. Just make sure that
heartbeat (a.k.a. SOE test) is enabled for the DS200 and DS100.

Getting rid of the DELNI may be a good idea all by itself. It is not exactly
new technology; it is DEC's excellent engineering standards that allow 20
years old equipment to be still in use. But the DELNI does things to
packets. IIRC it eats part of the preamble that keeps transceivers in sync
and nesting DELNI's was restricted and when connected to an ethernet segment
it reduced its length in certain configurations.

With an Allied Telesys UTP transceiver you can connect the DS200's straight
to your UTP switches (it may help to configure the switch ports manually to
10 Mb/s and half duplex mode). If there still is a Cisco router in the path
between DS200 and a host then you've got two options:
- bridge LAT on the Cisco
- make your own LAT-telnet application gateway (use reverse LAT on a DS300
or higher and bind the advertised LAT service with a port that autoconnects
to a TELNET service)

Hans




