Hi!

Here is an explanation without getting too far into the details - please
excuse my english.

This calibration procedure takes care that the head will hit a cylinder
perfectly in a very short time when needed. The disk�s onboard processor
determines some parameters to ensure this when it recalibrates. In
modern drives, the heads are not moved by a stepping motor but by a
coil. This is a drive that works linear and the disk�s processor needs
to have some real life parameters to hit the cylinder without moving to
far. The tracking fine adjustments are performed while reading a
cylinder.

In the older drives that had a stepping drive, there was a predetermined
number of steps from one cylinder to the next. Accesing the right
cylinder was easy; the disk controller just had to calibrate to cylinder
0 when the drive was powered up. After that, it just had to remember
where it was at every time. Recalibration just had to be performed when
the drive was not able to read a track - the drive usually just went to
cylinder 0 and did another try.

Frank.


