Well, I'm dont have experience with DEC hardware (other than my much
beloved vt420), but AFAIK most dec boxes had a serial interface to
connect a serial video terminal (like a vt100) or the like. They're to
be had cheap if you look around a bit.

If you can't find a serial terminal -- acually anything with a serial
interface will do -- connect an old laptop/notebook, or even your
desktop PC to the serial line via a null modem cable, boot he DEC box
and see if you receive something on the serial port (try 9600,8,1,n) to
begin with. Any terminal program will do (e. g. telix, minicom, etc.).

Maybe a (hardware) switch has to be flipped to enable a serial console.

Have a look at http://vt100.net/manx/ (great website!!!) for a manual to
find out how your box could be configured to provide a serial console.

Regarding parts I'd check ebay, or politely phone some companies in your
vicinity, whether they might have some forgotten DEC hardware buried in
their "electronics graveyard", they'd be willing to give away (chances
are you'd get it for a buck, a few bud's or even free ;).

Hope this helps,
so long,
