OK. The other post did not show up, hence the question.

Basically, powersupplies will need a certain output to work properly. A volt
meter is designed to present a very high resistance to the source. Any
current it draws will drop its reading, right.
If the load on the PS gets excessive, that is an upper current limit is
exceeded, then the voltage will drop in order follow Ohm's law. If you
connect several devices in parallel, each with its own internal restistance
then the "replacement" resistor is lower than the individual internal
values. Put enough devices in parallel and the total resistance offered to
the power supply is too low: V=I*R , say a maximum current of 20 A, so the
minimum value for R is 2.5 Ohms to end up at 5 Volts. Reduce R further, to
say 2.2 ohms and the current limiters kick in and the voltage drops to 4.4
Volts. Instead of a fixed voltage device the power supply becomes a slave of
its load.
So push the pins of your multimeter in the 5V lead while connect to the
drive. Switch on the Powersupply and observer the voltage. If it drops below
5 V you know what happens.




