That would be correct for a traditional, "linear" power supply. For
computers switch-mode power supplies are used, which have a different
behaviour -- or to be fair, there are two different characteristics.

Rated (nominal) current is 100%.

(1) Nominal output voltage is maintained up to about 110% of rated
current. If the load resistance drops further, i.e. the load draws more
current, the voltage drops down to zero at about 130% of rated current.

(2) Nominal output voltage is maintained up to about 110% of rated
current. If the load resistance drops further there is a "fold back" in
the U/I (voltage versus current) characteristic, i.e., the voltage drops
down to zero at about 20% of rated current. That is done to reduce the
internal heat dissipation of the power supply. -- There has to be a
"hysteresis" within the regulator's loop to prevent oscillation in the
case of an overload condition.

> So push the pins of your multimeter in the 5V lead while connect to the
> drive. Switch on the Powersupply and observer the voltage. If it drops below
> 5 V you know what happens.

