Cthulhu <spambox@rlyeh.it> writes:
> I'm searching a way to replace that QIC-80 like cartidge that the VAX
> 11/725 used to load microcode. The one we have is FBAU, and as you know
> without this tape that VAX is unusable.
> 
> Is there a way so, maybe using a standard QIC-80 tape reader connected
> to a Linux box, some can dump its content to let me try resurrect the
> ancient thing?
> 
> Is it really a QIC-80 tape, or anything something totally different?

It's a TU58 DECtape II, which is a DC100 with a proprietary format.  It
is not QIC-80, and AFAIK no other tape drives can read or write TU58
format.  It is also not possible to format (or reformat) TU58 tapes.
(Firmware for the TU58 drive to format tapes existed inside DEC but was
not made available to customers.)

The normal interface to a TU58 drive is a serial port, although a
parallel interface version also existed and was apparently used inside
some PDT models.  It is possible to hook up a serial TU58 drive to a PC,
or to use a PC to emulate a serial TU58 drive.


