On 4 Sep 2003 16:02:07 GMT, Wolfgang Rupp <spamtrap@coredump.at> wrote:

>Hans Vlems <hvlems.nieuw@zonnet.nl> wrote:
>> As long as you are sure all relevant VMS software lives below 1 GB then
>> a 3100 will happily boot from a 4 GB disk.
>
>How do I do that? I managed it with NetBSD, but not with VMS do far.

INIT the disk with the index at the beginning.  Then allocate the high part
of the device to a single large file (fdl will allow you to specify
placement).  Then install VMS or restore an image (/noinit) to the low 1Gb
area.  Afterwards, either delete the large file and never upgrade or defrag,
or use it as a container file for a virtual disk.  The latter is more
elegant as well as safer - mark the container file /nobackup and you will be
able to separately backup the system area and your virtual disk.  Restores
will require some manual intervention though.

-- 
John


