Subject says it all.

Actually, it probably doesn't matter if it's the console or not, and 
probably doesn't matter what type of system it is connected to.

I posted a while back on this question, and after I couldn't get 
anything to work, concluded that it is a cable problem.

I realised there are at least 50 types of serial cable: each end can be 
DEC 423 or, if not, then large or small and male or female.  That's 5 
possibilities times two ends is 25.  Multiply by 2 since the cable can 
be crossed or straight-through.

So, I tried out my cables.  I have about 6 different kinds (discounting 
whether or not they are straight-through or not).

The only cable I have which seems to work has a big DB25 at each end, 
one male and one female.  The Falco (in contrast to, say, a VT320) has a 
female connector.  So, I connected the male end to the Falco and the 
female end to a DB25-to-DB9 adapter.  (The ALPHAstation has male DB9's
for the serial ports; obviously, the adapter is female on both sides.)

This now seems to work.

The remaining problem is that the cursor isn't well behaved.  Sometimes, 
some of the output appears twice---where it should be, and somewhere 
else.  Things like MONITOR have some of the output misplaced.

There is nothing obviously wrong going by the output of SHOW TERMINAL.  
However, there seem to be things in the Falco setup which aren't 
indicated in SHOW TERMINAL.

Does anyone have a FALCO connected to a VMS machine and have it work 
with no problems?

Years ago, using the same terminal with the same machine, I seem to 
recall that it DID work fine, so I am thinking that my FALCO setup is 
not entirely correct.

It is a rather long cable, and in contrast to the situation several 
years ago, there are more "live" cables nearby.  Could it be a problem 
with line noise?



