Sounds more like a flow control problem to me.  Things like MONITOR
display the same text in the same place over and over.  If one of
the cursor positioning commands gets garbled due to dropped characters,
it will position to the wrong place, and you'll see just what you
are describing.  Does CTRL/W or CTRL/R repaint the screen correctly?
(These should do an "ERASE SCREEN" and then repaint it.  I think they
do slightly different things, but in a quick test with MON SYS, they
both seemed to work the same.)

Make sure both the terminal and port are set to the same flow-control
method (usually XON/XOFF for any historically DEC equipment, but I
think some of the newer stuff might support RTS/CTS, etc.)  If the
Falco has any delay or silo settings, you might try fiddling with
those.  If nothing else works, lowering the baud rate might help.

Or the Falco might just be flakey.

