I just fired up a VT 102 I have had for a few years but have never had 
in use (I collected it with some other used stuff, but had no room until 
now to actually hook it up---no, I didn't get rid of anything, but just 
moved some stuff from the computer room (the attic) in my flat to the 
computer room in another flat).  I just plugged it into a VAX and it 
worked.  

Having discovered yesterday that my VAXes will now tolerate the loss of 
the console (I have no idea why---it used to be that switching off the 
console terminal or merely disconnecting it (in hardware by pulling the 
cable, or in software by switching to the other port in the setup) would 
cause a reboot), I did the easiest thing, based on the cable I had lying 
around---I connected an MMJ via an adapter on the VT to the MMJ console 
port of a VAX.

Actually, I started typing this message on the VT 102, but then 
accidentally hit the BREAK key.  :-(  Is there a way to disable this in 
the SETUP of the VT 102?

OK, some keys are missing, but it's great how the keypad works with VMS 
MAIL and with my own defined keys in NEWSRDR and other apps.  Also, the 
highlighting etc due to escape sequences is really nice (like with a 
VT 320; it would be nice if a DECterm could make things BRIGHTER, not 
just fatter).

The beep of the terminal sounds really nice.  Also, the keys sound 
almost like a real typewriter---fantastic!!!

I notice that the size of the text decreases from the top of the screen 
to the bottom (I have an old VT320 where the size decreases from left to 
right, probably a similar problem).  Is there any way to adjust this, or 
is it just a symptom of age?  If the latter, can I expect it to get 
worse before things give out?  How much longer can I expect the thing to 
last?

I always am wondering for each piece of hardware---especially terminals 
and monitors---how much idle time is necessary before switching it off 
is the better option, i.e. the gain by not having it switched on is more 
than the loss caused by the stress of switching it off and on.  Any idea 
what that time would be for the VT 102?

Since the BREAK key on the VT 102 halts the VAX, should I expect the F5 
key on an LK 201 or LK 401 to do the same?



