XON/XOFF is in fact an important issue when using terminals: About two weeks 
ago I connected a VT420 to my Linux machine (no, I'm not collecting old 
VAXen ;-)). I remember I had to set flow control in the VT's setup to 
"XON/XOFF @ 64", otherwise the whole thing wouldn't work. Additionally, I 
use HW handshaking (RTS/CTS) via RS232C.

I'm not quite sure how the setup on a FALCON looks like, but I had a similar 
problem until I switched to "VT400 mode; 7-bit controls". Do not use 8-bit 
controls unless your host really supports them.

>> If the
>> Falco has any delay or silo settings, you might try fiddling with
>> those.  If nothing else works, lowering the baud rate might help.
> 
> It's only at 9600.
> 
In that case, you should not encounter too many bit errors. If the cable is 
longer than 3 meters, you should use a shielded cable. And don't forget to 
use a null-modem cable with the HW handshaking lines (RTS�CTS, DTR�DSR) 
also crossed over.

Good luck!
-- 
Jacek Ruzyczka



