Get a vacuum cleaner.  Unplug the VAX (stay grounded).  Unplug all cables
from all disks, terminators, and boards.  Vacuum the plugs, disks, boards,
sockets, terminators.  Clean the edge connectors with alcohol wipes - don't
touch them afterwards.  Put the cables back on, re-seat them once or twice.
Plug in the VAX.

Lay out your anti-static mat.  Kneel on it, facing Mecca (or the North Pole
if you believe in Santa).  Cross your fingers on both hands and bow down
low.  Pray.  Turn on the VAX and watch the lights.

This is the best first steps, as any DEC FE will tell you.  If you still
have the Red Light of Death, then you are probably SOL.  However, if you can
find a hammer, proceed to step 2.

Step 2 is to remove the suspect disk drive and hold it parallel to the
floor.  Note the round area that houses the platters.  Hold the disk drive
so that the spindle is perpendicular to the center of the earth.  Without
striking your fingers or any circuit board edge connectors, take aim and
whack that disk drive in the corner about as hard as you would strike a nail
when hanging a picture.  If a platter is stuck to a head, this might be
enough to jar it loose.  Stick it back in the VAX and repeat the prayer
part.  Turn it on and watch for lights.  If still bad, check eBay for a new
disk drive.  Note that if you have data to preserve, there is a slight
possibility that the problem is with the disk controller board on the drive,
and you can swap that out independently of the Head-Disk Assembly.  This
only fixes things 1% of the time though.


