took several power cycles and numerous incantations to make it go.  So
then we have a power cut, of course (yes, there's a UPS, but it doesn't
hold up for 16 hours!)

Power cycles and incantations proving ineffective, I took the drive out
and tried the usual rapid-twist trick.  Followed by the sharp-tap
technique.  No go.  The drive was trying, but failing, to start.  Out of
the office, downstairs and out the back to the workshop, and put it on a
big PSU, capable of 12A at 14V (5V logic suppied from a separate PC
PSU).  However, even the extra voltage wasn't *quite* enough.

Ok, nothing to lose, take the cover off.  Oh look, there's a mechanical
brake.  A paper clip held that out the way, and it nearly started. 
Eventually by giving it a spin with my finger, I got the platters turning.

But it still won't restart normally if I let it spin down (guess how I
found out).  Now how do I get this upstairs and back into the computer to
retrieve the data, without it spinning down?  Don't try this at home...

I yanked the wires supplying the 14V from the bench PSU and stuffed the
proper 12V from the PC power supply back into place without it spinning
down.  Then I found a small UPS, yanked the mains lead from the PC PSU,
and stuffed in one from the UPS.  Carefully lifted the PSU onto the UPS,
lifted the drive onto the PSU, taped them together, and carried the whole
lot upstairs.

I got all the data off without an error :-)

-- 
Pete						Peter Turnbull
						Network Manager
						University of York


